// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET
//
// Registry #930, the second receiver kind — and the one that shows what the missing
// check actually cost.
//
// `"x".try_to_i16()` also passed the checker before 2026-09-05, then died in the C
// compiler: `passing 'nova_int' to parameter of incompatible type 'nova_str'`. A
// checker that accepts a program the backend cannot build is worse than one that
// refuses it, because the error surfaces in generated code the author never wrote.
//
// `str` is carried as its own fixture rather than folded into the `f64` one because
// the two travel different paths to the same site: `f64` is a numeric primitive with
// intrinsics of its own sitting beside the blanket (which is why `f64.abs()` must stay
// legal — see the positive), while `str` has none of that and reaches the check with
// nothing else claiming the name. The narrowing predicate got `str` WRONG on the first
// attempt: `str` has a `type str` declaration in the prelude, so "is this a declared
// type?" re-admitted exactly this line. Measured, then fixed.
//
// Spec: D310 (`spec/decisions/02-types.md`). Positive half:
// `spec_tests/conformance/p930_blanket_set_bound_pos.nv`.
module neg.p930_blanket_set_bound_str_neg

fn ask_a_string_for_an_integer_conversion() Io -> () {
    ro s = "x"
    // nova:expect E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET -- `str` is not in `Ints`; before the fix this passed the checker and failed in the C compiler instead
    println(s.try_to_i16().is_ok())
}
